  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 217 PM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: High pressure remains overhead this afternoon with some \nchanneling enhanced winds in the Champlain and St. Lawrence Valley. \nWinds will continue to remain breezy in the wider valleys through \ntonight, especially across northern New York. A low-level jet \nassociated with a broad area of low pressure centered well north \ninto the Hudson Bay will bring continued southwest flow to the St. \nLawrence Valley overnight. Beyond sunset, typical radiational \ncooling process will lower temperatures towards freezing in the \nNortheast Kingdom and near the Connecticut River under clear skies. \nAcross northern New York, the low level jet will likely stifle \nradiational cooling conditions with increased flow aloft as well as \nadvecting some warmer air, keeping lows in the low to mid 40s for \nthe St. Lawrence Valley and the low to mid 30s for most of the \nAdirondacks. South to southwest flow will continue to bring warmer \nair into the region with highs tomorrow into the low 60s. A weak \nmoisture starved cold front will slide south from Canada Monday \nnight into Tuesday and wash out over the area with no precipitation \nexpected, though some morning clouds and cooler temperatures can be \nexpected Tuesday morning. Sunny skies will prevail thereafter with \nlarge scale ridging leading to low humidity and light winds through \nmid week. There continues to be high confidence of no precipitation \nareawide at least through mid week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: A classic stretch of fall weather will continue late \nthis week and through the weekend. Surface high pressure will build \nsouth out of Quebec on Thursday and Friday, bringing cold air \nadvection. Highs will drop from the mid-50s to lower 60s on Thursday \ninto the upper 40s to around 50 on Friday. The high should be \ncentered almost directly overhead over the weekend, leading to clear \nskies, light winds and highs in the 50s. Most mornings will be \nfrosty during this stretch, with lows in the 20s and 30s.",
